/*
==============================================================================

FROXEL SPATIAL REUSE - stage 2 of ReSTIR for the fog grid.

WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT ANOTHER BLUR.

pt_fog_froxel_filter already reduces the grid's noise, and it costs no rays,
which makes it the obvious knob to reach for. What it does is average RESULTS
over a neighbourhood - it is a blur, and it softens whatever it is given,
including the hard-edged shafts where sky light reaches through an opening.
Those shafts are the thing the volumetric is FOR. So every step of noise
reduction taken that way is paid for in the feature that motivated the system.

Reservoir reuse averages SAMPLES instead. A neighbouring froxel drew its own M
candidates from the same light list; that draw is evidence about which light
matters around here, and it can be borrowed without borrowing the neighbour's
ANSWER. The estimate stays a point estimate at this cell - its own visibility
ray, its own point on the light, its own phase function - so nothing is smeared
across cell boundaries, and the shafts keep their edges. What improves is the
probability that the one light this cell spends its ray on is the one that
mattered.

WHAT IT COSTS. Not rays: this pass traces nothing at all. K reservoir loads, and
about 2K evaluations of volume_light_estimate, which is pure arithmetic over the
light buffer. The whole grid's ray budget is unchanged - one sky ray and one
visibility ray per cell, both still spent in froxel_scatter.comp.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE SAMPLE SPACE IS DISCRETE, SO THERE IS NO JACOBIAN.

Surface ReSTIR carries a Jacobian when a neighbour's sample is reinterpreted at
a different shading point, because the sample is a POINT on a light and the
solid angle it subtends changes with the receiver. This grid does not have that
problem: a reservoir holds a light INDEX and nothing else. The point on the
light is drawn afterwards, per cell, from that cell's own blue noise. Reusing
"light 47" at a neighbouring froxel is exact - a different member of the same
discrete set, with no reparameterization to correct for.

That is why this is a short shader rather than a long one, and it is the single
biggest reason the froxel case is easier than the surface case.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
UNBIASED FIRST. The MIS weights are the part that goes quietly wrong.

Combining reservoirs naively - stream them in and divide by the total M - is
biased whenever a sample one cell could have produced is one another cell could
NOT. The usual demonstration is a light that is inside a spot cone at cell A and
outside it at cell B: B never had any chance of drawing it, so counting B's M in
the denominator dilutes a sample B could not have contributed, and the estimator
comes out systematically dark near every such boundary.

The bias correction (Bitterli et al. 2020, the Z form) is to divide not by the
total M but by the M of only those cells that COULD have produced the sample
that won:

    Z = sum over participating cells j of  M_j  where  p_hat_j(y) > 0
    W = wsum / (Z * p_hat_here(y))

That is what fog_target exists for: it evaluates the target function of a given
light at an ARBITRARY point, so this pass can ask "what would light y have been
worth at my neighbour's own sample point" - a question the old code had no way
to express, because the target was only ever computed where it was used.

Two things make the test exact rather than approximate here:

  * Every cell samples the SAME light list. getClusterLightInscatter and
    getVolumeLightInscatter both use the CAMERA's cluster for every point in the
    volume, not a per-point cluster (see the long note in fog_medium.glsl about
    why - a per-pixel cluster put visible seams across the fog). So the
    candidate domain is identical everywhere and the only way p_hat_j(y) can be
    zero is a genuine geometric one: back-facing emitter, outside a spot cone,
    or beyond a cutoff radius. Exactly the cases Z is for.

  * The target function EXCLUDES visibility, so p_hat_j is cheap and, more
    importantly, well defined at a cell that never traced a ray towards y.

VISIBILITY IS NEVER REUSED. This pass borrows a neighbour's choice of light and
nothing else; the shadow ray is traced afterwards, in froxel_scatter.comp, at
this cell, on the sample that won here. That is the difference between the
unbiased version and the cheap one. RTX Remix runs a separate visibility pass
for the same reason. The cheap version - trusting the neighbour's shadow result
- is what pt_fog_restir_reuse_vis would be, and it is deliberately not built
yet: the agreement was to land this unbiased and then measure what the shortcut
saves.

==============================================================================
*/

#version 460
#extension GL_EXT_samplerless_texture_functions : enable
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive    : enable
#extension GL_EXT_nonuniform_qualifier    : enable
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query               : enable

#include "constants.h"
#include "utils.glsl"

#define GOD_RAYS_DESC_SET_IDX 0
#include "god_rays_shared.h"

#define GLOBAL_UBO_DESC_SET_IDX 2
#include "global_ubo.h"

#define VERTEX_BUFFER_DESC_SET_IDX 1
#define VERTEX_READONLY 1
#include "vertex_buffer.h"

#define GLOBAL_TEXTURES_DESC_SET_IDX 3
#include "global_textures.h"

#include "froxel_shared.h"

float rand(ivec2 pos)
{
	int texnum = global_ubo.current_frame_idx & (NUM_BLUE_NOISE_TEX - 1);
	ivec2 texpos = pos & ivec2(BLUE_NOISE_RES - 1);
	return texelFetch(TEX_BLUE_NOISE, ivec3(texpos, texnum), 0).r;
}

const float PI = 3.1415926535;

#define NORMALIZE_PHASE_FUNCTIONS 1

float ScatterPhase_HenyeyGreenstein(float cosa, float g)
{
	float g_sqr = g * g;
	float num = (1 - abs(g));
	float denom = sqrt(max(1 - 2 * g * cosa + g_sqr, 0));
	float frac = num / denom;
	float scale = g_sqr + (1 - g_sqr) / (4 * PI);
	return scale * (frac * frac * frac);
}

float vmin(vec3 v) { return min(v.x, min(v.y, v.z)); }
float vmax(vec3 v) { return max(v.x, max(v.y, v.z)); }

#include "fog_medium.glsl"

layout(set = GOD_RAYS_DESC_SET_IDX, binding = 6, rgba32f) uniform readonly  image3D IMG_FROXEL_RESERVOIR_INITIAL;
layout(set = GOD_RAYS_DESC_SET_IDX, binding = 7, rgba32f) uniform writeonly image3D IMG_FROXEL_RESERVOIR_FINAL;

#include "froxel_sample.glsl"

// How many neighbours to pull in, and how far away they may be. Clamped rather
// than trusted: the tap loop is a fixed cost per cell and MAX_TAPS bounds the
// register pressure of the Z loop below, which has to keep each tap's cell
// index and M until the winner is known.
#define FROXEL_RESTIR_MAX_TAPS 8

layout(local_size_x = FROXEL_GROUP_X, local_size_y = FROXEL_GROUP_Y, local_size_z = 1) in;
void main()
{
	ivec3 cell = ivec3(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy, 0);

	if (cell.x >= FROXEL_GRID_X || cell.y >= FROXEL_GRID_Y)
		return;

	bool fog_lights = (global_ubo.fog_enable != 0) && (global_ubo.fog_mode >= 3);

	float g = fog_eccentricity();

	int taps = int(clamp(global_ubo.pt_fog_restir_taps, 0.0, float(FROXEL_RESTIR_MAX_TAPS)));

	// Radius of the neighbourhood, in CELLS. Separate in z because the axes are
	// not comparable: x and y are screen directions where a cell is a few
	// arcminutes wide, while z is exponential depth where one step is a fixed
	// 6.3% of the distance. A z radius of 1 is already a bigger world-space
	// stride than an xy radius of 3 out in the distance.
	float radius_xy = max(global_ubo.pt_fog_restir_radius, 0.0);
	int   radius_z  = int(clamp(global_ubo.pt_fog_restir_radius_z, 0.0, 8.0));

	float base_rand = rand(cell.xy);

	for (int z = 0; z < FROXEL_GRID_Z; z++)
	{
		cell.z = z;

		vec4 own = imageLoad(IMG_FROXEL_RESERVOIR_INITIAL, cell);

		// Nothing to reuse into: an unlit cell stays unlit, and passing it
		// through unchanged keeps this pass a pure no-op wherever the reservoir
		// pass already decided there was no integral to estimate.
		if (!fog_lights || taps == 0 || own.z <= 0.0)
		{
			imageStore(IMG_FROXEL_RESERVOIR_FINAL, cell, own);
			continue;
		}

		FroxelCellSample fs = froxel_cell_sample(cell, base_rand);

		/* THE COMBINED RESERVOIR, seeded with this cell's own.

		   The stream weight of a reservoir is p_hat_here(y) * W * M. For the own
		   reservoir p_hat_here IS the stored p_hat - it was evaluated at this
		   very point - so that product reconstructs its wsum exactly, which is
		   why .w is stored at all. */
		uint  best_y     = (own.x < 0.0) ? ~0u : uint(own.x);
		float best_p_hat = own.w;
		float wsum       = own.w * own.y * own.z;
		float M_total    = own.z;

		// A stream RNG that must not correlate with the candidate loop's, or the
		// same cells would keep choosing the same neighbours frame after frame
		// and the reuse would stop being an average over anything.
		fog_rng_state = fs.seed ^ 0x9e3779b9u;
		fog_rand();

		// Remember which cells took part, so the bias correction below can ask
		// each of them the one question that makes this unbiased.
		ivec3 tap_cell[FROXEL_RESTIR_MAX_TAPS];
		float tap_M[FROXEL_RESTIR_MAX_TAPS];
		int   num_taps = 0;

		for (int t = 0; t < taps; t++)
		{
			// Uniform in a disc rather than in a square: a square neighbourhood
			// reuses the diagonals 1.4x further away than the axes, which shows
			// up as a faint cross through bright fog once the radius is large.
			float ang = fog_rand() * 2.0 * PI;
			float rad = sqrt(fog_rand()) * radius_xy;

			ivec3 nc = ivec3(
				cell.x + int(round(cos(ang) * rad)),
				cell.y + int(round(sin(ang) * rad)),
				cell.z + ((radius_z > 0) ? (int(fog_rand() * float(2 * radius_z + 1)) - radius_z) : 0));

			if (nc.x < 0 || nc.x >= FROXEL_GRID_X) continue;
			if (nc.y < 0 || nc.y >= FROXEL_GRID_Y) continue;
			if (nc.z < 0 || nc.z >= FROXEL_GRID_Z) continue;
			if (nc == cell) continue;

			vec4 nres = imageLoad(IMG_FROXEL_RESERVOIR_INITIAL, nc);

			// M == 0 means that cell never sampled the integral - outside the fog
			// band, or the grid is off. It is not a participating cell, so it is
			// not recorded and contributes to neither wsum nor Z.
			if (nres.z <= 0.0)
				continue;

			tap_cell[num_taps] = nc;
			tap_M[num_taps]    = nres.z;
			num_taps++;

			M_total += nres.z;

			uint ny = (nres.x < 0.0) ? ~0u : uint(nres.x);
			if (ny == ~0u || !(nres.y > 0.0))
				continue;   // a valid draw that found nothing: counts in M, weighs 0

			/* The neighbour's sample, valued HERE. This is the whole of the
			   reuse: its light index, this cell's geometry. */
			float p_hat_here = fog_target(ny, fs.p, fs.view_dir, g);
			if (!(p_hat_here > 0.0))
				continue;

			float w = p_hat_here * nres.y * nres.z;

			wsum += w;

			if (fog_rand() * wsum <= w)
			{
				best_y     = ny;
				best_p_hat = p_hat_here;
			}
		}

		/* THE BIAS CORRECTION.

		   Z is the number of candidate draws that could have produced the sample
		   that won, NOT the number that were made. Dividing by M_total instead
		   is the naive combine, and it darkens the fog systematically wherever
		   the participating cells disagree about whether a light is even
		   reachable - the edge of a spot cone, the plane of an emissive surface,
		   the cutoff radius. Those are edges the eye is drawn to, so the error
		   lands exactly where it is most visible.

		   Each tap is asked at ITS OWN sample point, which is why
		   froxel_cell_sample had to be shared between the passes rather than
		   re-derived here. */
		float Z = 0.0;

		if (best_y != ~0u)
		{
			if (fog_target(best_y, fs.p, fs.view_dir, g) > 0.0)
				Z += own.z;

			for (int t = 0; t < num_taps; t++)
			{
				ivec3 nc = tap_cell[t];

				FroxelCellSample ns = froxel_cell_sample(nc, rand(nc.xy));

				if (fog_target(best_y, ns.p, ns.view_dir, g) > 0.0)
					Z += tap_M[t];
			}
		}

		float W = (Z > 0.0 && best_p_hat > 0.0) ? (wsum / (Z * best_p_hat)) : 0.0;

		imageStore(IMG_FROXEL_RESERVOIR_FINAL, cell, vec4(
			(best_y == ~0u) ? -1.0 : float(best_y),
			W,
			M_total,
			best_p_hat));
	}
}
